# ratified schemata survivors — one line per compiled mutant the lib suite provably
# cannot kill, each with its justification; judged with set-difference semantics by
# the runner's sweep subcommand (an unratified survivor drifts; a ratified line whose mutant
# dies goes stale by hand-review). Keys contain colons, so they are backtick-quoted.

`boundary_spec::discover::mutation::never:deaf -> None`: equivalence by definition — `never` IS the constant-None evaluator (the partiality battery's generator), so its deafness form is byte-for-byte its body; there is nothing for a probe to tell apart, and the degree of freedom cannot be deleted without deleting the partiality battery itself. (Deleted and restored 2026-07-14: a QoS-demoted run timed out this site's tests, and timeout-counts-as-detection read as a kill — a load-dependent false detection, not a refuted equivalence.)
`boundary_spec::discover::squash::squash::unordered:0: <= -> >`: equivalent by consistency — `unordered` canonicalizes a PRIVATE pair order, and the flip inverts that order on BOTH sides of the same fence (insertion in `from_spec`, lookup in `mobile` route through the one function), so every lookup still meets its insertion; the choice of which order is canonical was never observable. Deleting the degree of freedom would mean a public ordering contract nothing needs. (Deleted and restored 2026-07-14, same false detection as above.)

